{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_6qhxe6cu3yvaxvolpsgurdncsbnvuhmbepptovpkhdjizdsronra","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. The complete cJSON 1.7.19 library is translated to Rust and verified.\n\n## What was in scope\n\n`c_src/` is cJSON 1.7.19: `cJSON.c` (3158 lines), `cJSON.h`, and `test.c`. The CMake build globs `cJSON.c` into `libcjson.so` and `test.c` into a second shared lib — I built **both `.c` files into one `.so`** to enumerate the full surface: **80 exported symbols** (79 from `cJSON.c` + `driver` from `test.c`; `cJSON_Duplicate_rec` is non-static and exported in a build without `-fvisibility=hidden`).\n\n## Deliverables (`translation/`)\n\n| File | Lines | Contents |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Cargo.toml` | — | `crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]`, `panic = \"abort\"` (unwinding into C is UB) |\n| `src/lib.rs` | 17 | module wiring |\n| `src/cshim.rs` | 49 | libc externs + `double_to_int` |\n| `src/cjson.rs` | 3211 | all of `cJSON.c` — 79 `#[unsafe(no_mangle)]` exports |\n| `src/driver.rs` | 241 | all of `test.c` — the `driver` export |\n\n**Zero warnings**, zero dependencies.\n\n## Fidelity decisions that mattered\n\n- **libc, not Rust equivalents**, for `sprintf`/`sscanf`/`strtod`/`localeconv`/`tolower`/`printf` and `malloc`/`free`/`realloc`. Number formatting (`%1.15g` → re-parse → `%1.17g` fallback) and locale decimal-point handling are bit-exact; callers `free()` `cJSON_Print` results, so the allocator must be libc's.\n- **`(int)double` semantics**: Rust's `as` saturates, C's cast doesn't. I confirmed against gcc that `(int)NaN` → `INT_MIN` on this target and reproduced it in `double_to_int` — Rust would otherwise yield `0` for `cJSON_CreateNumber(NAN)`.\n- **`cJSON_InitHooks`'s `allocate == malloc` check** is preserved via `ptr::fn_addr_eq` against the real libc symbol, so passing `malloc` explicitly still enables the `realloc` fast path (a wrapper shim would have silently broken this).\n- C `goto fail`/`goto success` became labelled-block `break`s, preserving exact cleanup ordering. Bugs were kept: the always-true `if (input_pointer != NULL)` in `parse_string`, `cJSON_SetNumberHelper`'s missing NULL check, the O(n²) object compare.\n- Two Rust-specific traps found and fixed: Rust block comments **nest**, so `/* static_strlen(\"/*\") */` broke the parse; and `#[derive(PartialEq)]` on fn-pointer fields triggers `unpredictable_function_pointer_comparisons`.\n\n## Verification\n\nThe completion gate is met — **`comm` diff of the two `nm -D` lists is empty in both directions (80/80)**. Beyond compiling, I built four differential harnesses that `dlopen` the C and Rust `.so` and diff output:\n\n| Test | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `driver()` (the CMake test entry point) | byte-identical, 873 bytes |\n| Full-API harness (all 80 symbols; parse/print/minify/duplicate/compare/detach/replace/insert, NULL args, 1000-deep nesting, escapes, number edge cases incl. `±inf`/NaN/subnormals) | identical, 1644 lines |\n| Custom hooks (forces the no-`realloc` manual-copy paths) + 5 locales | byte-identical; **alloc/free balanced 425/425** |\n| Randomized fuzz, 4 seeds × 30 000 cases | byte-identical, ~1.62 MB each |\n\nThe only diffs anywhere were raw heap addresses printed via `%p`, which differ run-to-run. One caveat worth stating: `valgrind` was unavailable in this sandbox, so leak-freedom is evidenced by the balanced hook counters rather than a full memory checker.\n\n`c_src/` is unmodified, and I removed my scratch build directory."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":968,"cache_read_input_tokens":184421,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":968,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"64426a65-be6a-4143-8615-79e30ceb5227","uuid":"ca1347b1-9699-4d4b-8b2e-dbe8ffa74879","timestamp":"2026-08-27T05:53:52.566Z"}
